          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            By
            %em cohesive homotopy type theory
            one will mean a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            implementing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+theory"} cohesive homotopy theory
            via an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
                cohesive
                = succeed "-toposes" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            A first formulation of cohesive homotopy type theory &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#SchreiberShulman2012"}> Schreiber &amp; Shulman (2012)
            \&amp;rbrack; added the required
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            to plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            Another approach &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Shulman15"}> Shulman (2015)
            \&amp;rbrack; is to change the underlying rules of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            itself by adjoining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            for
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            (“crisp contexts”).
          %p
            This second approach, via a modified type theory with crisp contexts (which has meanwhile be implemented in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistants"} proof assistants
            such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda-flat"} Agda-flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs"} proofs
            internal to the theory and is what most authors now mean by (real-)cohesive homotopy type theory, see e.g. developments in:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Myers19"} Myers (2019)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Myers21"} Myers (2021)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MyersRiley23"} Myers &amp; Riley (2023)
          %p
            In any case,
            %em Cohesive homotopy type theory
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+theory"} cohesive homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
          %p
            In its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            in cohesive HoTT are interpreted as
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} cohesive ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+cohesive+toposes"} motivation for cohesive toposes
            for a non-technical discussion.
          %h2#TheAxioms The Axioms
          %p
            We discuss the formulation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            of the
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos#InternalDefinition"} internal axioms
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            Cohesive homotopy type theory is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            which adds to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; \dashv \flat \dashv \sharp
          %p called
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊣
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊣
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+%28in+computer+science%29"} monads
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            is an idempotent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
          %h3#a_codiscrete_objects A) Codiscrete objects
          %p
            %strong Axiom A.
            %em
              The ambient
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} left-exact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} reflective sub-(∞,1)-category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} base (∞,1)-topos
              “of
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+objects"} codiscrete objects
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            code at
            %a{:href => "https://github.com/mikeshulman/HoTT/blob/master/Coq/Subcategories/Codiscrete.v"} Codiscrete.v
          %p We write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \sharp X
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflector"} reflector
            into codiscrete objects.
          %p
            The homotopy type theory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+objects"} codiscrete objects
            we call the
            %em external
            theory.
          %h3#b_discrete_objects B) Discrete objects
          %p
            %strong Axiom B.
            There is also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} coreflective sub-(∞,1)-category
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+objects"} discrete objects
            such that with the codiscrete reflection it makes the ambient theory that of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} local (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            code at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/mikeshulman/HoTT/blob/master/Coq/Subcategories/LocalTopos.v"} LocalTopos.v
          %p The coreflector from discrete objects we write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\flat A \to A
                  \\,.
          %h3#c_cohesion C) Cohesion
          %p
            %strong Axiom C
            The discrete objects are also reflective, the reflector is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            to the coreflector and preserves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            code at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/mikeshulman/HoTT/blob/master/Coq/Subcategories/CohesiveTopos.v"} CohesiveTopos.v
          %p We write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Π
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \to \Pi X
          %p
            for the reflector into
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+objects"} discrete objects
          %h2#as_a_multimodal_type_theory As a multimodal type theory
          %p
            There is another way to define cohesive homotopy type theory, as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multimodal+type+theory"} multimodal type theory
            %a{:href => "#GKNB21"} Gratzer, Kavvos, Nuyts, &amp; Birkedal 2021
            and consists of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em mode theory
            %em modes
            and whose 1-cells are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em modalities
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \in \mathcal{M}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            in the type theory, which comes with its associated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+lock"} context lock
          %h3#the_mode_theory_of_cohesion The mode theory of cohesion
          %p
            The mode theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            of cohesive homotopy type theory consists of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                two modes, the crisp or discrete mode
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒹
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{d} \in \mathcal{M}
                and the cohesive mode
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒸
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℳ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{c} \in \mathcal{M}
            %li
              %p
                1-cells
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo !
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo *
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒸
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒹
