          %p
            which makes the 1-cells into an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+quadruple"} adjoint quadruple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_! \dashv p^* \dashv p_* \dashv p^!
          %p
            The notations for the adjoint quadruple are derived from the introduction of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman15"} Shulman 2015
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Disc
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Codisc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi \dashv \mathrm{Disc} \dashv \Gamma \dashv \mathrm{Codisc}
          %p
            (i.e. see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+infinity-topos"} cohesive infinity-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            conflicts with the use of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo .
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi x:A.B(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            conflicts with the use of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            to express arbitrary contexts in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            endo-modalities on the cohesive mode can be defined as composites of the modalities above:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒸
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒸
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒸
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒸
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mo !
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒸
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp \coloneqq p_* \circ p^!:\mathcal{c} \to \mathcal{c} \qquad \flat \coloneqq p_* \circ p^*:\mathcal{c} \to \mathcal{c} \qquad \esh \coloneqq p_! \circ p^*:\mathcal{c} \to \mathcal{c}
          %p
            yielding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \esh \dashv \flat \dashv \sharp
          %h3#the_modal_type_theory The modal type theory
          %p In the multimodal type theory associated with cohesive homotopy type theory, there are two type judgments:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \; \mathrm{type} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a type in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \; \mathrm{type} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a type in the cohesive mode.
          %p Similarly, there are two term judgments:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is a term of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is a term of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in the cohesive mode.
          %p and two context judgments:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a context in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a context in the cohesive mode.
          %p
            as well as two separate judgments each for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            of types and terms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \equiv B \; \mathrm{type} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                are judgmentally equal types in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \equiv B \; \mathrm{type} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                are judgmentally equal types in the cohesive mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b:A \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                are judgmentally equal terms of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b:A \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                are judgmentally equal terms of type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in the cohesive mode.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \equiv \Delta \; \mathrm{ctx} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{d}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                are judgmentally equal contexts in the crisp mode;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒸
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \equiv \Delta \; \mathrm{ctx} \; \mathrm{at} \; \mathcal{c}
                which says that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
                are judgmentally equal contexts in the cohesive mode;
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#GKNB21"} original papers
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multimodal+type+theory"} multimodal type theory
            use the symbol @ instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{at}
            but it doesn’t seem to be possible to put @ inside of latex math mode on the nLab, whether directly or inside the mathrm command.
          %p
            Then we have the rules for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+locks"} context locks
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            of cohesive homotopy type theory:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} at
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒹
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} lock
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
