          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#GKNB21"} original papers
            also used a lock symbol that the author does not know how to replicate on the nLab.
          %p (…)
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#KS23"} Kolomatskaia &amp; Shulman 2023
            might also be useful here in defining the type theory)
          %h2#example_phenomena Example phenomena
          %p Before looking at the consequences of the axioms formally, we mention some example phenomena to illustrate the meaning of the axioms.
          %h3#GeometricSpacesAndTheirHomotopyTypes Geometric spaces and their cohesive homotopy types
          %p
            We indicate one central aspect of geometric homotopy theory that is not visible in plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            The standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} topological spaces
            plays two rather different roles, depending on what kind of equivalence between spaces is considered. To make this more vivid, it serves to think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            as equipped even with its canonical structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"}> with boundary
            ).
          %p
            The canonical map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to *
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            is certainly not a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            the interval carries non-trivial structure. Notably its endpoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0,1 : I
            are not equivalent points (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"}> terms
            ) in differential geometry, but are distinct. From the point of view of differential geometry the interval is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 0-type
            (has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level"} h-level
            2) – but one that is in some way equipped with geometric structure.
          %p
            This geometric structure, however, induces also a notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} geometric paths
            in the interval, such that any two of its points are connected by such a path, after all. In other words, one can form the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(I)
            of the interval and regard
            %em that
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %em without
            further geometric structure (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} discrete ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(I)
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            itself is not.
          %p
            As such, the canonical map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(I) \to *
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            after all, namely a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-groupoid"} (-1)-type
            and actually a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-2%29-groupoid"} (-2)-type
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level"} h-level
            0) – up to equivalence the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
          %p
            This latter property is what makes the interval important in bare
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder+objects"} cylinder objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopies"} left homotopies
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
          %p
            In cohesive homotopy type theory these two roles of the interval can both be seen, via the reflective embedding of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+objects"} discrete objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid+in+a+locally+infinity-connected+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} fundamental ∞-groupoid reflector
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}
          %p
            Specifically, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} homotopy cohesion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"}> with boundary
            ) are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} fully faithfully embedded
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
            exists as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            that behaves as the interval in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(I)
            is equivalent to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
          %p
            More generally, in this model every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy 0-type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-truncated
            object, but the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(X)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} discrete ∞-groupoid
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            is that of the topological space underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of topological spaces.
          %p
            In particular, the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            in this model is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated"} 0-type
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(S^1)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-truncated"} 1-type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
          %p
            One can turn this around and axiomatize a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+object"} line object
            in cohesive homotopy type theory as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{A}^1
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{A}^1) \simeq *
          %h2#Structures Structures in cohesive homotopy type theory
          %p
            We discuss implications of the axioms of cohesive homotopy type theory and go through the discussion of the various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures+in+a+cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structures in a cohesive (∞,1)-topos
          %h3#cohomology Cohomology
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            two
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sharp(X \to A)
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to A
            is the
            %em
              type of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level"} h-level
            2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated"} truncation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_0 \sharp(X \to Y)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#flat_cohomology_and_local_systems Flat cohomology and local systems
          %p
            We give the
            = succeed "-formalization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+--+structures#FlatDifferentialCohomology"} Flat cohomology and local systems
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            with coefficients in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat A
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %em flat cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo :
                  %mo ♯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c : \sharp(X \to \flat A)
            is called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+system"} local system
            of coefficients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p (…)
          %h3#de_rham_cohomology de Rham cohomology
          %p
            We give the
            = succeed "-formalization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity,1%29-topos+--+structures#deRhamCohomology"} intrinsic de Rham cohomology
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \mathbf{B}G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-connected"} connected
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            type of the coreflection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}G
            we call the
            %em de Rham coefficient type
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ♭
                      %mi dR
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat_{dR} \mathbf{B}G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+sequence"} fiber sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi dR
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mo ♭
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\flat_{dR} \mathbf{B}G \to \flat \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}G
                  \\,.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"}> Rocq
            \-code:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                Require Import Homotopy Subtopos Codiscrete LocalTopos CohesiveTopos.
                
                Hypothesis BG : Type.
                Hypothesis BG_is_0connected : is_contr (pi0 BG).
                Hypothesis pt : BG.
                
                Definition flat_dR : #Type
                  := ipullback ([[fun _:unit => pt]]) (from_flat ([BG])).          %h3#differential_cohomology Differential cohomology
          %p
            We give the
            = succeed "-formalization" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+--+structures#DifferentialCohomology"} Differential cohomology
          %p (…)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real-cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} real-cohesive homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A1-homotopy+type+theory"} A1-homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractured+homotopy+type+theory"} fractured homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+type+theory"} geometric homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modal homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable homotopy type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+homotopy+type+theory"} differential homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
          %h2#References References
          %p
            First discussion of a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"}> homotopy
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theoretic
            formulation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+theory"} cohesive homotopy theory
            (adopting terminology from
            %a{:href => "cohesive+topos#LawvereAxiomatic"} Lawvere 2007
            ) considered in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"} differential cohomology in a cohesive topos
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7930"}> arXiv:1310.7930
              )
          %p was given in
          %ul
            %li#SchreiberShulman2012
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Quantum+gauge+field+theory+in+Cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} Quantum gauge field theory in Cohesive homotopy type theory
              %strong 158
              (2014) 109-126 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1408.0054"}> arXiv:1408.0054
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.158.8"} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.158.8
          %p following
          %ul
            %li#ShulmanInternalizing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              %em Internalizing the external, or The Joys of Codiscreteness
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2011/11/internalizing_the_external_or.html"}> blog post
              )
          %p
            by adding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            to plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p See also broader discussion in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Modern+Physics+formalized+in+Modal+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} Modern Physics formalized in Modal Homotopy Type Theory
                (2016)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Corfield"}> David Corfield
                , Chap. 5 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/davidcorfield/show/Modal+Homotopy+Type+Theory"} Modal Homotopy Type Theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/modal-homotopy-type-theory-9780198853404"} ISBN:9780198853404
          %p
            Another type-theoretic formulation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+theory"} cohesive homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            notion of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type"} modal
            (“crisp”)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
          %ul
            %li#Shulman15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Brouwer’s fixed-point theorem in real-cohesive homotopy type theory
              %strong 28
              6 (2018) 856-941 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.07584"}> arXiv:1509.07584
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129517000147"} doi:10.1017/S0960129517000147
          %p
            following a general pattern for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            laid out in
          %ul
            %li#LicataShulman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjoint logic with a 2-category of modes
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://lfcs.info/lfcs-2016/"} Logical Foundations of Computer Science 2016
              %strong 9537
              (2016) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-27683-0_16"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-319-27683-0_16
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/ls15adjoint/ls15adjoint.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/ls15adjoint/ls15adjoint-lfcs-slides.pdf"} slides
          %p with exposition in:
          %ul
            %li#Shulman19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Comonadic modalities and cohesion
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://felix-cherubini.de/modal-workshop.html"} Geometry in Modal Homotopy Type Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://home.sandiego.edu/~shulman/papers/cmu2019a.pdf"} pdf slides
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/GA93Hjh-Alk"} talk recording
          %p This approach (also “real cohesive type theory”) is now what most people refer to when speaking of cohesive homotopy type theory.
          %p
            Notice that at this point there is no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
            that actually implements the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            this way, only the system consisting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
            (
            %em>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+type+theory"} spatial type theory
            ) runs on computers: eg. via
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda-flat"} Agda-flat
          %p
            Discussion of a fragment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohesive
            homotopy type theory with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda-flat"} Agda-flat
          %ul
            %li#Wellen18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Wellen"}> Felix Wellen
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/thesis+Wellen"} Cartan Geometry in Modal Homotopy Type Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.05966"}> arXiv:1806.05966
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.kit.edu/iag3/~wellen/media/diss.pdf"} thesis pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Cherubini"}> Felix Cherubini
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Synthetic
                    = succeed "-jet-structures" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129524000355"} doi:10.1017/S0960129524000355
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.05966"} arXiv:1806.05966
          %p Further development of (real-)cohesive homotopy type theory:
          %ul
            %li#Myers19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Good Fibrations through the Modal Prism
              %strong 6
              1 (2022) 212–255 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.08034"}> arXiv:1908.08034v2
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://higher-structures.math.cas.cz/api/files/issues/Vol6Iss1/Myers"} higher-structures:Vol6Iss1/Myers
          %p
            Formalization of the shape/flat-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+square"} fracture square
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"}> differential cohomology hexagon
            ):
          %ul
            %li#Myers21
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jaz+Myers"}> David Jaz Myers
                ,
